Why is anonymized or synthetic data insufficient?

Aquí es donde más debes justificar.

Anonymized or synthetic data cannot be used because the analysis requires validating specific production records associated with the reported incident. The issue is related to the correct ingestion and persistence of data fields originating from real SWIFT PACS messages.

To identify the root cause, it is necessary to verify the exact values stored in the production database and compare them with the expected data from the original transaction flow. Synthetic or anonymized data would not replicate the exact conditions of the reported issue.

Why will the use of real data directly contribute to achieving a business objective?

Access to real production data will allow verification of whether the affected fields are correctly received, stored, and propagated through the system workflow. This enables identification of the specific step in the processing pipeline where the data is lost or incorrectly populated.

Resolving this issue ensures correct processing of SWIFT transactions and proper population of data in the Sentinel system, which is necessary for operational integrity and compliance monitoring.

Are there potential alternatives to using real data?

Aquí debes explicar que no realmente.

No. The incident is related to specific production transactions and requires verification of the actual records stored in the database. Alternative approaches such as synthetic data or development environment testing would not reproduce the exact conditions or transaction identifiers involved in the incident.

Therefore, reviewing the actual production records through controlled read-only queries is the only reliable way to perform the analysis.

Responsible for the elimination of real data

Aquí puedes aclarar que no se extraerán datos, solo se consultan.

No data extraction, replication, or storage outside the production environment will occur. The activity consists only of executing read-only SQL queries to validate specific records related to the incident. Therefore, no additional data handling or deletion process is required

The exercise requires executing two controlled read-only SQL queries in the production database to validate specific records associated with an incident related to data population in the Sentinel system.

The objective is to verify whether certain fields originating from SWIFT PACS messages are correctly stored in the database and to identify at which stage of the system workflow the data may be missing or incorrectly populated.

The queries will be limited to specific transaction identifiers associated with the reported incident. No bulk extraction of customer data or historical datasets will be performed.
